#########
# DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE
# Managed by Ansible
######
# Removes the OpenNMS packages this role pins from `unattended-upgrades`
# consideration. It exists because the APT preferences file next to it cannot
# express what the pinning design actually wants to say.
#
# What it wants: prefer exactly opennms_version, and if that version is gone,
# do nothing. APT preferences cannot state that. A -1 on every version would,
# but it leaves the package with no installation candidate and breaks a
# deliberate `apt install` — see the preferences template's own warning. So the
# preferences file compromises twice, and each compromise is a version an
# unattended host will move to on its own:
#
#   tier 1 matches `<version>-*`, so any Debian revision of the configured
#     release is admitted at 1001. A reissued -2 is selected and the service
#     restarts, on a day nothing in this playbook changed.
#   tier 2 matches the major at 900, so once the configured version leaves the
#     repository a newer patch is selected.
#
# Both were measured, not assumed. With o=OpenNMS admitted to the
# unattended-upgrades origin allowlist, `unattended-upgrade --dry-run` selected
# 35.0.5-2 in the first case and 35.0.6-1 in the second.
#
# Package-Blacklist is a gate separate from candidate selection, so it stops
# both without touching installability. `apt-get install <pkg>=<version>` still
# installs through it, which is why this role's own versioned install and a
# deliberate opennms_version bump are unaffected.
#
# The cross-major protection does NOT come from here. That is the preferences
# file's -1 tier, which was measured to govern unattended-upgrades on its own.
# This file changes nothing about it.
#
# Entries are anchored at the end with `$`. unattended-upgrades matches these
# with re.match, so the start is anchored implicitly and the trailing anchor is
# the load-bearing half: without it `opennms-common` would also match a future
# `opennms-common-extras` that nobody decided to blacklist.
#
# One file per role by default, named for the role. APT accumulates list
# entries across apt.conf.d rather than letting the last definition win, so a
# single-host stack running several OpenNMS roles gets the union of their
# packages. A shared filename would instead mean whichever role ran last
# decided the contents, silently dropping the other's packages -- which is
# what happens if opennms_unattended_blacklist_file is overridden once in
# group_vars for a host running more than one of these roles. Override it per
# role, or leave the defaults alone.
#
# Set opennms_unattended_upgrade_blacklist: false to remove this file. That
# re-exposes both moves described above.
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
{% for pkg in opennms_pinned_packages %}
    "{{ pkg }}$";
{% endfor %}
};
